# rfc9552-52-rs-opaque-withdraw-peer-down -- the departing client's opaque routes go away.
#
# RFC requirement: RFC9552-5.2-8 positive -- "An implementation MUST handle unknown
# Link-State NLRI types as opaque objects and MUST preserve and propagate them."
# (RFC 9552 Section 5.2). Propagation is not only the announcement: a route the
# route server relayed and never withdraws is a route the consumer behind it
# believes forever, so the withdrawal is part of what "propagate" owes.
#
# WHAT IT PROVES, THROUGH THE DAEMON. Two route-server clients establish. The
# source announces one BGP-LS UPDATE holding two Link-State NLRIs, type 1 (Node)
# and type 99, which ze parses as neither. The receiver is sent both. The source
# then DEPARTS, and the receiver is sent an MP_UNREACH_NLRI naming both NLRIs
# byte-identically.
#
# PREVENTS: the defect fixed by 408b8386c. wireu.ParseNLRIs hands a family with
# no dedicated parser back as one *nlri.WireNLRI, whose String() is a size
# summary ("wire[bgp-ls/bgp-ls](23 bytes)") carrying none of the bytes. The route
# server pasted that summary into "update text nlri bgp-ls/bgp-ls del ...", the
# text parser answered route.ErrFamilyNotSupported, and every other client kept
# the departed peer's Link-State routes announced forever.
#
# WHY THIS FILE AND NOT rfc7606-54-bgpls-override-propagates.ci. That test cannot
# catch the defect on any platform: it was run with the fix disabled and still
# passed. Its assertions stop at the ANNOUNCEMENT, and no route-server .ci ever
# closed a peer, so the withdrawal path had no functional coverage at all. Unit
# tests (rs/server_opaque_withdrawal_test.go) prove the command the route server
# builds; only a running daemon proves the bytes reach the other client
# (ai/rules/interop-and-goal-validation.md).
#
# THE CLOSE IS REACHABLE, WHICH IS NOT FREE. One ze-peer holds ONE cursor over
# its (conn, seq) groups: nextCloseAction (internal/test/peer/checker.go) fires
# only when "close" is the head of the CURRENT group, and a group advances only
# when every rule in it is consumed. So an unmet assertion anywhere below the
# close is a deadlock, not a failure. Two consequences shape this file. The
# source and the receiver are SEPARATE ze-peer processes, so the receiver's wire
# assertions cannot hold the source's cursor. And the source's own seq=2 is a
# rule the OBSERVER satisfies on demand (a route injected to the source alone),
# so the cursor always reaches seq=3.
#
# THE ORDERING IS EARNED, NOT TIMED, AND BOTH ENDS OF IT ARE NEEDED. The source
# announces only after the ROUTE SERVER itself reports both clients up, and
# departs only after ze has written the receiver the announcement. The observer
# then holds the daemon up until the withdrawal has been written.
#
# The first of those two barriers was added after a load run reproduced its
# absence on invocation 8 of 40 (scripts/dev/stress-repro.py): the source's own
# session came up first, it announced immediately, and the route server dropped
# the UPDATE with
#   "forward matched no target ... why=127.0.0.2=not-yet-up, 127.0.0.1=source".
# Nothing recovers that UPDATE in this fixture, because peer-up replay reads
# bgp-adj-rib-in and no adj-rib-in is loaded here: "peer-up replay gave up
# waiting for adj-rib-in to reach the cut ... ingested=0". The window is wide on
# darwin, where two peers need 15s or more to establish when one binds 127.0.0.2.
#
# The barrier polls "show bgp rs peers", which is the ROUTE SERVER's own view of
# which peers it will forward to (peerStatus, rs/server_handlers.go), not the
# reactor's session state. The two are set in different places and the gap
# between them is exactly what the load run fell into.
#
# THE FOUR ADDRESSES ARE DISTINCT ON PURPOSE. Each peer's remote and local
# addresses differ, because originatedNextHopIsPeerOwn
# (internal/component/bgp/reactor/forward_next_hop.go) WITHHOLDS a route whose
# NEXT_HOP is the destination peer's own address, with no exemption (RFC 4271
# Section 5.1.3). The observer's barrier route is originated by ze, so a peer
# block naming one address on both ends would swallow it and the source would
# never reach its close. test/plugin/relay-withdraw-nexthop-self.ci carries the
# long-form reasoning.
#
# GRACEFUL RESTART IS DISABLED ON BOTH SESSIONS. With it negotiated ze acts as a
# helper and RETAINS the departed peer's routes as stale, which is the correct
# behaviour and the opposite of what this file asserts.

# Source client (ASN 65001) on 127.0.0.1. It waits, announces, waits again, and
# departs. Both waits are BARRIERS the observer satisfies on demand: neither is
# an assertion about ze, and neither can hold the cursor below the close forever.
stdin=source:terminator=EOF_SOURCE
option=tcp_connections:value=1
option=timeout:value=60s
# seq=1 -- BARRIER ONE. 192.0.2.0/24 (18 C0 00 02), injected to THIS peer alone
# once the route server reports BOTH clients up. Announcing before that is the
# race the load run found: the UPDATE is dropped and nothing replays it.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:contains=18C00002
# seq=2 -- one UPDATE, two Link-State NLRIs.
#   ORIGIN:   40010100                 IGP
#   AS_PATH:  40020602010000FDE9       AS_SEQUENCE[65001], 4-byte ASNs
#   MP_REACH: 800E20 4004 47 04 01010101 00   AFI 16388 / SAFI 71, next-hop 1.1.1.1
#     NLRI 1: 0001 000B 0200000000000000000000     type 1, the Node type
#     NLRI 2: 0063 0004 DEADBEEF                   type 99, a type nothing registers
# ze parses NEITHER of them here. The default branch of wireu.ParseNLRIs has no
# dedicated parser for bgp-ls, so the whole 23-octet section arrives as ONE
# *nlri.WireNLRI. The payload carries TWO of them so the fixture covers the SPLIT:
# appendOpaqueRecords divides that one blob into one record per NLRI with
# message.GetNLRISizeFunc, and the withdrawal below has to name both.
action=send:conn=1:seq=2:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF004702000000304001010040020602010000FDE9800E204004470401010101000001000B020000000000000000000000630004DEADBEEF
# seq=3 -- BARRIER TWO. 198.51.100.0/24 (18 C6 33 64), a DIFFERENT prefix from
# barrier one so neither can satisfy the other's rule. The observer injects it
# only after ze has written the receiver the announcement. It is what makes seq=4
# reachable: see "THE CLOSE IS REACHABLE" above.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=3:contains=18C63364
# seq=4 -- depart. A TCP close with no NOTIFICATION, which is what a client that
# died looks like to the route server.
action=close:conn=1:seq=4
EOF_SOURCE

# Receiving client (ASN 65002) on 127.0.0.2 -- both wire assertions live here.
stdin=receiver:terminator=EOF_RECEIVER
option=tcp_connections:value=1
option=timeout:value=60s
# This peer must not leave before the observer has seen the withdrawal written. A
# receiver that closes first turns a missing withdrawal into a connection-closed
# error, which names neither assertion.
option=linger:value=true
# seq=1 POSITIVE -- the whole MP_REACH_NLRI arrives byte-identical, the unknown
# type in place. Asserting from the attribute header rather than from the NLRI
# section alone is what keeps this rule and seq=2 apart: the withdrawal below
# carries the same 23 NLRI octets under a different attribute code, and a needle
# that named only those octets would be satisfied by either frame.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:contains=800E204004470401010101000001000B020000000000000000000000630004DEADBEEF
# seq=2 NEGATIVE -- the departure withdraws both NLRIs. An EXACT frame, not a
# substring, because every field of it is part of the claim:
#   0034        message length 52
#   0000        withdrawn-routes length 0 -- the withdrawal is not IPv4 unicast
#   001D        total path attribute length 29
#   800F 1A     MP_UNREACH_NLRI, optional, 26 octets of value
#   4004 47     AFI 16388 / SAFI 71
#   then the SAME 23 octets the source announced, type 99 included and unchanged.
# Without the fix this frame never arrives at all: the withdrawal command the
# route server built named "wire[bgp-ls/bgp-ls](23 bytes)" and was refused before
# any byte reached this peer.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=2:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF0034020000001D800F1A4004470001000B020000000000000000000000630004DEADBEEF
EOF_RECEIVER

# Observer: makes the departure and the shutdown wait on STATE, never on a clock.
tmpfs=bgpls-withdraw.run:mode=755:terminator=EOF_RUN
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Drive the source client: announce when the route server can forward, then leave."""
import json
import sys

from ze_api import API, dispatch, result_json_data, runtime_fail

SOURCE = '127.0.0.1'
RECEIVER = '127.0.0.2'


def rs_peers_up(api):
    """Addresses the ROUTE SERVER itself counts as up, as a set.

    This is peerStatus (internal/component/bgp/plugins/rs/server_handlers.go)
    reporting PeerState.Up, which handleState writes under rs.mu in the same
    critical section that makes the peer a forward target. The reactor's own
    session state is a DIFFERENT fact set in a different place, and forwarding
    reads this one.

    Empty on any transient -- a command that has not completed, a payload of
    another shape -- so a poller keeps waiting instead of raising. The guarded
    module-level dispatch is used for the same reason (see API.peer_fields).
    """
    result = dispatch(api, 'show bgp rs peers') or {}
    result = result.get('result', result)
    if result.get('status') != 'done':
        return set()
    try:
        payload = result_json_data(result, {})
    except (json.JSONDecodeError, TypeError, ValueError):
        return set()
    if not isinstance(payload, dict):
        return set()
    return {
        row.get('address')
        for row in payload.get('peers', [])
        if isinstance(row, dict) and row.get('up')
    }


def receiver_updates(api):
    """Real UPDATEs written to the receiver, End-of-RIB markers excluded.

    updates-sent counts EVERY UPDATE written to the peer, the initial-sync EoR
    included (IncrUpdatesSent in reactor/reactor_notify.go sees only the message
    type). eor-sent is the separate marker counter, so the difference is the
    relayed traffic this test is about.
    """
    return (api.peer_counter('updates-sent', peer=RECEIVER)
            - api.peer_counter('eor-sent', peer=RECEIVER))


def barrier(api, prefix):
    """Release one of the source client's waits.

    The route reaches the SOURCE alone, so it never disturbs the receiver's
    UPDATE count. nhop 1.1.1.1 is neither peer's own address, so
    originatedNextHopIsPeerOwn does not withhold it (RFC 4271 Section 5.1.3).
    """
    api.send('peer ' + SOURCE + ' update text origin igp nhop 1.1.1.1'
             ' nlri ipv4/unicast add ' + prefix)


def main():
    api = API()
    api.declare_done()
    api.wait_for_config()
    api.capability_done()
    api.wait_for_registry()
    api.ready()

    # BARRIER ONE. Both clients must be forward targets IN THE ROUTE SERVER's
    # own view before the source announces. On darwin this is the slow step: 15s
    # or more when one peer binds 127.0.0.2.
    if not api.wait_until(lambda: rs_peers_up(api) >= {SOURCE, RECEIVER}, attempts=100):
        runtime_fail('the route server never reported both clients up')
    print('OK: the route server reports both clients up', file=sys.stderr)
    barrier(api, '192.0.2.0/24')

    # quiesce FIRST so the poll behind it is a safety net rather than the
    # mechanism: it drains the forward pool in one RPC.
    api.quiesce()
    if not api.wait_until(lambda: receiver_updates(api) >= 1, attempts=60):
        runtime_fail('the receiver was never sent the BGP-LS announcement')
    print('OK: receiver was sent the BGP-LS announcement', file=sys.stderr)

    # BARRIER TWO releases the departure.
    before = receiver_updates(api)
    barrier(api, '198.51.100.0/24')

    # Hold the daemon up until the withdrawal has actually been written to the
    # receiver. Counting from `before` rather than from zero keeps the barrier
    # routes above from satisfying this poll: both went to the source only.
    if not api.wait_until(lambda: receiver_updates(api) > before, attempts=60):
        runtime_fail('the receiver was never sent the BGP-LS withdrawal')
    print('OK: receiver was sent the BGP-LS withdrawal', file=sys.stderr)

    api.dispatch('request shutdown')
    api.wait_for_shutdown()


if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()
EOF_RUN

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
	internal rs {
		use bgp-rs
	}
	external bgpls-withdraw {
		run ./bgpls-withdraw.run
		encoder json
	}
}
bgp {
	peer source-peer {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.3
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65001
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
				bgp-ls/bgp-ls { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
		attach process rs {
			receive [ update-received state open-received refresh ]
			send [ update ]
		}
		attach process bgpls-withdraw {
			send [ update ]
		}
	}
	peer receiver-peer {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.2
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.4
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65002
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			rs-client true
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
				bgp-ls/bgp-ls { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
		attach process rs {
			receive [ update-received state open-received refresh ]
			send [ update ]
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze-peer --port $PORT:stdin=source
cmd=background:seq=2:exec=ze-peer --bind 127.0.0.2 --port $PORT:stdin=receiver
cmd=foreground:seq=3:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=60s
expect=exit:code=0
reject=stderr:pattern=panic recovered
reject=stderr:pattern=fatal error
reject=stderr:pattern=ZE-OBSERVER-FAIL
